- Security (heartbeat): the reply is read up to 256 KB (decoded) and 60 s, with `Accept-Encoding: identity`. A hostile server, or a man in the middle on http or a Local server, could otherwise send a gzip bomb and run a 32-bit box out of memory.
- Fix (settings): a section with the wrong type in a hand-edited data/settings.json falls back to its default, with one warning. `"web": null` made the supervisor fail on every start, so no engine ran at all; `"remote": null` stopped heartbeats until the engine restarted; `"remote": "x"` with UPTIME_API_KEY set broke every process. Also `"enabled": "yes"` and the like.
- Fix (supervisor): an engine that stops itself because a check hung (exit 5) is restarted after 1 s and is not counted as a crash. Before, it got the crash backoff, so while one target hung every monitor on the box missed about a minute of pushes every few minutes, and during an update trial it counted toward a rollback.
- Fix (supervisor): one failing loop iteration (a full disk, a file Windows has locked, a child that cannot be started on a box out of memory) is logged and the loop goes on. Before, the supervisor exited and stopped its healthy children with it. A child that cannot be started is retried with the usual backoff.
- Fix (supervisor): the stalled-engine check measures how long run/status.json has not changed by the monotonic clock. Before, it compared the file time with the wall clock, so w32time stepping the clock (a dead CMOS battery) killed a healthy engine, or hid a stall.
- Fix (self-healing): on a full disk, when run/status.json cannot be written, the engine still touches it. Before, the supervisor read the missing update as a stall and killed a healthy engine about every 4 minutes.
- Fix (self-healing, update): a new version whose supervisor keeps stopping before its 5-minute trial ends is rolled back after the 4th start. Before, only engine crashes were counted, so a release with a broken supervisor was restarted by the OS forever.
- Fix (engine): checks that had not started when the cycle ran out of time are cancelled and logged as skipped. Before, the old thread pool ran them late, next to the next cycle's pool: more threads than max_workers, and a false DOWN then UP in Kuma.
- Fix (logs): a thread that dies (watch, heartbeat, a log writer) is logged CRITICAL with its traceback. Before, it left no trace: pythonw has no stderr.
- Fix (files, Windows): writing a JSON file retries for a moment when Windows refuses the rename because another process has the file open (the web reading status.json, an antivirus scan).
- Fix (server log level): a level set by the server is written to data/settings.json once. Before, it was written again on every heartbeat until the next check cycle.
- Fix (self-healing, logs.db): after a corrupt logs.db was moved aside, every process kept writing into the moved file (Linux, macOS): the Logs page stayed empty, and the moved file grew with no retention or size cap. Each writer now notices that the file was replaced and opens the new one. On Windows the move failed while the other processes had the file open, so retention and the 200 MB cap stopped for good; now each writer is asked to let go and moves the file itself.
- Fix (logs.db): old lines are deleted in batches of 10,000. One big DELETE at the size cap grew the -wal file by tens of MB on a box short of disk and held the lock long enough for the other processes to write to the fallback file.
- Security (Local network server): "on this network" is an explicit list of ranges (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, 127/8, 169.254/16, 100.64/10, ::1, fc00::/7, fe80::/10). Python's is_private, used before, also counts ranges routed on the internet (Teredo 2001::/32) or never used on a LAN (198.18/15, 240/4, 0/8) as private, so the certificate of such a server was not checked.
- Fix (Settings): the form's limits come from the same table that data/settings.json is checked against, and a value outside them is an error instead of being changed silently. Before, the form allowed less than the file (check interval up to 3600, workers 50, log days 365, heartbeat 3600): on a box with `"check_interval": 7200`, modern browsers refused to save the page at all, and IE8/9 saved 3600 without a word. Now: check every 10-86400 s, parallel checks 1-64, keep logs 1-3650 days, heartbeat 30-86400 s. The monitor form shares its limits with monitors.json the same way.
- Performance (Logs page): WARNING and up reads each level from its index in id order and merges them: 1-2 ms on a million rows instead of about 400 ms, which on a Windows 7 disk was seconds, during an outage, when the page is needed most.
- Performance (checks): an HTTP/HTTPS monitor whose host name does not resolve is down at once, without a second lookup by requests (which the DNS circuit breaker cannot cut short). Through a proxy the request is still made, since the proxy resolves the name.
- Security (sign-in): a password attempt is counted before the hash is checked. Before, it was counted after, so many requests sent at the same moment all passed the 10-per-5-minutes limit. At most two password hashes are computed at a time (each costs about half a second of CPU, more on a Win7 Atom), the list of addresses tracked is capped at 2000, and a hash in users.json with an absurd round count is refused instead of hanging the web UI.
- Security (Account page): a wrong current password counts toward the same limit, per account. Before, a stolen session cookie could try current passwords without limit.
- Security (install, Windows): the check of who may write to the base Python also catches a single user account, such as the owner of a Python installed "for me only" under C:\Users. The service runs that python as SYSTEM, so that user could take over the box. `main.py install` warns and says to install Python for all users.
- Fix (engine): a heartbeat in flight for over 10 minutes restarts the engine (exit 5, no crash count). requests' timeout is per read: a server that trickles its reply, or a hung TLS handshake, held the heartbeat thread, and with it every restart or update the server sent.
- Fix (engine): an update downloads and installs on a thread of its own. Before, an install clicked in the web UI ran on the watch thread, so stop requests and the orphan check waited for the download; an offered update ran on the heartbeat thread, so no heartbeat went out meanwhile.
- Fix (engine): when monitors.json cannot be read for a moment (an antivirus or backup tool holds it), the engine keeps checking the monitors it has and tries again next cycle, with one warning. Before, a read error looked like an empty file and every monitor stopped until the next change.
- Fix (dashboard): the tiles say the same thing when the page opens and after the 15 s refresh. Before, the first paint showed "On" and full timestamps, and the refresh changed them to "OK" and "since <date>". The server now writes the words once (views.tile_text) for both.
- Fix (dashboard): the uptime percentage comes from the engine's memory (run/status.json). Before, it was read from history.json, which is written every 10 cycles, so it lagged by up to 10 minutes.
- Fix (CLI): `main.py logs --level CRITICAL` (and WARN) filters as asked. The Logs page no longer offers a "cli" process: CLI commands print to the console and never wrote to logs.db.
- Performance (Logs page): a text search reads at most 100,000 lines per page, and Older searches the next 100,000. LIKE cannot use an index, so a rare word read the whole of logs.db every time (about 0.1 s per million lines here, seconds on a Win7 disk); now about 10 ms.
- Fix (logs): a push that fails is one WARNING line, with the host and the error. The same failure again is DEBUG, with a reminder once an hour ("still failing: N in a row"), and the first push that works again says after how many failures. Before, every failed push wrote an ERROR and a WARNING line on every cycle: 5 monitors whose Uptime Kuma was down wrote about 29,000 lines a day.
- Fix (logs): a Local server on plain http logs "plain http, not encrypted" instead of "a self-signed certificate is accepted".
- Security (packages): requirements.txt carries the SHA-256 of every file of every pinned package, so pip installs in hash-checking mode: a package changed on PyPI, a mirror or a proxy is refused instead of installed and run as SYSTEM. `tools/pin-hashes.py` rewrites the hashes after a pin changes, and `--check` resolves the set for Windows, Linux and macOS on Python 3.8 to 3.14 and fails on any package that is not pinned.
- Fix (packages): colorama is pinned on Windows. click needs it there; before, Windows boxes got whatever colorama was newest on the day they installed.
- Security (packages): Python 3.10 and newer get requests 2.34.2 and urllib3 2.8.0; Python 3.9 and newer get Flask 3.1.3, Werkzeug 3.1.9 and waitress 3.0.2. These fix the 2025-2026 CVEs in urllib3 (decompression bombs, chunked responses, proxies), Werkzeug (Windows device names in safe_join), Flask (Vary: Cookie) and requests.
- Security (Python 3.8 / Windows 7): the fixed releases do not install on 3.8, so the agent defends itself.
  - Response bodies (heartbeat reply, update download) are read without urllib3's decompression or chunked-response parser, and inflated up to a fixed limit. Kuma pushes and HTTP checks read no body at all.
  - Redirects are followed by the agent and their bodies are never read. requests read, and inflated, the whole body of a redirect even with allow_redirects=False: one 302 with a 64 KB gzip body took 128 MB.
  - The web UI answers 404 to a URL naming a Windows device (NUL, CON, COM1.txt, ...), which on Windows hung a thread reading it (the Werkzeug fix).
  - waitress 3.0.0 gets the 3.0.1 fix for CVE-2024-49769: a client closing its connection early no longer leaves a socket in a busy loop. CVE-2024-49768 needs request lookahead, which stays off.
  - Every page sends Vary: Cookie (the Flask fix).
  - requests' CVE-2026-25645 applies only when requests runs from a zip file, which the agent does not.
- Security (users): users.json.bak is now a copy of the current users.json. Before, it was the version before the last change, so after a password change it still held the old password hash, and healing a corrupt users.json from it restored the old password, deleted users and sessions that had been signed out. On a box updated to 4.0.9 the backup is refreshed at the next sign-in.
- Performance (logs): each process's log writer wakes every 5 s when idle instead of twice a second. Closing it is still immediate.
4.0.8
2026-11-05
- Security (update): the agent no longer downloads an update from the reporting server unless `update.allow_server_download` is true in data/settings.json. Only the GitHub release asset is accepted by default. The web UI cannot set this flag. Before, an admin session could point `remote.url` at a server it controlled, and that server could offer its own zip with its own SHA-256. The agent would then run that code as SYSTEM.
- Security (monitors): `${UPTIME_SECRET_KEY}`, `${UPTIME_API_KEY}`, `${UPTIME_REMOTE_URL}` and `${UPTIME_SYNC_SECRET_KEY}` are never expanded in a monitor's push URL, monitor URL or headers. The monitor form rejects them. Before, anyone who could edit monitors could send the agent's HMAC key to any host.
- Security (setup): `/setup` also checks that the Host header is 127.0.0.1, localhost or ::1. A web page on another domain whose name resolves to 127.0.0.1 (DNS rebinding) can no longer create the admin.
- Security (install, Windows): `main.py install` makes Administrators the owner of every file in the install folder. The user who unzipped the release could otherwise make a .py file writable again. It also warns when ordinary users can write to the base Python folder that .venv runs on, and prints the icacls command that fixes it.
- Security (packages): Python 3.9 and newer get waitress 3.0.2 (CVE-2024-49769) and urllib3 2.6.3 (2025 CVEs). Python 3.8 keeps waitress 3.0.0 and urllib3 2.2.3, the last releases that install there.
- Security (logs): a failed push and the "most pushes returned 404" warning show only scheme://host. Before, the error text carried the full push URL with its token. The status and monitor lists no longer show user:password@ from a push URL either.
- Self-healing (data files): a JSON file that exists but does not parse is no longer treated as missing.
  - It is moved to `<file>.corrupt-<time>` (the last 3 copies are kept) and logged at CRITICAL.
  - settings.json, monitors.json and users.json keep a `.bak` of the last good version, and a corrupt file is restored from it.
  - If users.json is lost, `/setup` stays closed (otherwise whoever came first would become admin). The login page says to run `main.py reset-password`.
- Self-healing (logs.db):
  - A corrupt logs.db is moved to `logs.db.corrupt-<time>` and a new one is started. Before, every log line went to the fallback file for ever and the daily cleanup crash-looped the engine.
  - The old-row cleanup really gives the space back now. `PRAGMA incremental_vacuum` only works when its rows are read, and they were not.
  - logs.db is capped at 200 MB: the oldest lines are removed down to 150 MB, whatever their age.
  - Cleanup runs every hour, not once a day. With less than 200 MB free on the disk, only 3 days of logs are kept.
  - A full or read-only disk no longer stops a process from starting: logging falls back to the text file, then to stderr, then to nothing.
- Self-healing (update): the update state is written as "Applying" before any file moves. If the power goes or the supervisor is killed during an install, the next start puts the previous version back exactly as it was, then marks that version Failed.
- Self-healing (engine):
  - A check still running from an earlier cycle is reported down, and is not left showing its last "up".
  - A check stuck for 3 intervals makes the engine restart itself (exit code 5). A thread stuck in a call that never returns cannot be stopped any other way.
  - The supervisor restarts an engine that is alive but has not finished a cycle for 3 intervals + 60 s. It checks the age of run/status.json.
- Fix (settings): numbers in settings.json are checked when they are loaded. A value that is not a number, or is out of range, is replaced by the default or the nearest limit, with one warning. Examples: `"check_interval": "abc"`, `"max_workers": 999`, a port of 0. Before, a hand-edited value like these crash-looped the engine.
- Fix (autostart): a second install on the same box gets its own task, unit or launchd name (`UptimeSync4-<8 hex>`). Before, it took over the first install's autostart. The chosen name is kept in data/autostart.json.
- Fix (checks):
  - The DNS breaker is truly half-open. After 60 s, one check tries again while the others keep failing fast, and a failed retry reopens it at once.
  - HTTP(S) dns_ping is measured before the request. Measured after, the OS cache made it read about 0 ms.
  - "ping cannot run" and unexpected check errors are logged at ERROR once, with the traceback, and then at DEBUG.
- Fix (push): after a 404 streak, the first 200 no longer triggers the 404 warning again.
- Performance:
  - A push that succeeds with no change from the last one is logged at DEBUG. Only changes and errors are logged at INFO and above.
  - The Logs page uses new indexes for the level and process filters.
  - history.json is written compactly every 10 cycles and on stop, not with indentation every cycle.
  - On Windows, the ACL of the install folder is set again only when it has changed. Setting it on every start rewrote every file under .venv.
  - The supervisor takes its lock before it opens logs.db or reads settings, so the Windows minute trigger costs almost nothing.
  - The heartbeat keeps one connection to the server. The push connection pool grows with max_workers.
  - A warning (once) when the timeouts and retries cannot fit into check_interval.
  - The dashboard no longer fetches the status again as soon as the page loads.
- Code: every external program (ping, icacls, schtasks, systemctl, launchctl, pip, the update import test) runs through `procs.run` with a hard timeout. Before, autostart commands had none. Other cleanups: one User-Agent constant, one free-space limit, atomic PID files that cannot crash a process, and monotonic waits in the CLI.
- Correction to 4.0.6: an update that was put back leaves no empty backup folder. It counted towards the 3 kept backups, not 5.
- Tests: 59 new tests (tests/test_selfheal.py), covering the log store, heartbeat.send, the DNS query and breaker, push tracking, `_act_on`, autostart names and files, the supervisor backoff and pulse, `mask_push_url`, and every fix above.

