4.0.4
2026-11-04
- Fix (old browsers): the web UI now works in Internet Explorer 8 to 11, the browsers Windows 7 ships with. On a real Windows 7 32-bit box (IE10) the page looked broken.
  - Every response sends `X-UA-Compatible: IE=edge`. Without it, IE treats localhost and LAN addresses as intranet sites and renders them as IE7.
  - CSS: each `var(--x)` now has a plain colour just before it, which IE uses. Flex, grid and gap moved into `@supports (display: grid)`, which IE skips. IE gets a float/inline-block layout instead.
  - app.js rewritten in ES3: XMLHttpRequest instead of fetch, per-element handlers with an attachEvent fallback, no classList, closest, forEach or textContent-only code. The dashboard rows are rebuilt node by node, because IE9 and older cannot set innerHTML on a tbody. The script moved to the end of body and is no longer `defer`.
  - Templates use `div` with a `role` instead of main/header/nav/footer/section. IE8 cannot style HTML5 elements, and IE9-11 do not know `main`.
  - Modern browsers render as before: checked in Chrome, light and dark, and with the `@supports` block removed to preview what IE gets.
  - New OldBrowserTests guard these rules: var fallbacks, flex/grid only inside @supports, no HTML5 sectioning tags, the X-UA meta, and no ES5+ tokens in app.js. They fail against the 4.0.3 files.

